.TH AIDEBUG 1 "August 2026" "aidebug 1.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
aidebug \- AI-assisted malware reverse-engineering debugger
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aidebug
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
AIDebug performs static and optional dynamic malware-analysis triage. It emits
deterministic findings, analyst-review YARA candidates, versioned JSON with
heuristic IOC strings, and HTML reports. Optional remote analysis can suggest
MITRE ATT&CK mappings that require analyst validation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-binary " " FILE
Analyze a PE or ELF binary.
.TP
.BR \-\-source " " FILE.c
Compile one C translation unit to a temporary, non-executed ELF inside a
Bubblewrap filesystem sandbox, then analyze that artifact statically.
.TP
.B \-\-decompile
Use Ghidra headless to produce bounded C-like reconstruction for discovered
functions. The output is not recovered original source.
.TP
.BR \-\-decompile-all " " FILE.c
Decompile every discovered function and write one combined, provenance-marked
C-like file. The destination must not already exist.
.TP
.BR \-\-ghidra-headless " " PATH
Use PATH as Ghidra's support/analyzeHeadless launcher. This option requires
.B \-\-decompile
or
.BR \-\-decompile-all .
.TP
.BR \-\-mode " " MODE
Analysis mode: static, Frida dynamic tracing, or active local ELF debug.
.TP
.BR \-\-breakpoint " " LOCATION
Set an initial symbol/address breakpoint in active debug mode. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug-arg " " VALUE
Pass one argument to the debug target. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug-command " " COMMAND
Run a non-interactive active-debug command. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-gdb " " PATH
Use PATH as the GDB executable for active debug mode.
.TP
.BR \-\-learn " " [TOPIC]
List 44 bundled local lessons, open a lesson ID, or search a category/title.
.TP
.BR \-\-pid " " PID
Attach dynamic mode to an existing process ID.
.TP
.BR \-\-frida-host " " HOST[:PORT]
Use a remote Frida server in an operator-managed analysis target.
.TP
.B \-\-no-tui
Use CLI output instead of the terminal interface.
.TP
.B \-\-offline
Keep analysis local, use deterministic findings, and never call remote AI.
This option is also available as
.BR \-\-no-ai .
.TP
.BR \-\-max-functions " " N
Bound the number of functions processed by a bulk analysis (default 25, maximum
300). Dynamic mode uses the lower of N and its 50-function instrumentation cap.
.TP
.B \-\-accept-ai-cost
Acknowledge remote-data handling and possible provider charges for bulk remote
analysis. This flag is not authorization to send evidence off host.
.TP
.B \-\-list-sessions
List stored analysis sessions.
.TP
.BR \-\-session " " ID
Select a stored session for report, YARA, or JSON generation.
.TP
.B \-\-report
Generate an HTML report.
.TP
.B \-\-yara
Generate YARA candidate rules for high-risk findings.
.TP
.B \-\-json-export
Export analysis data as JSON.
.TP
.BR \-\-out-dir " " DIR
Write report output to DIR.
.TP
.BR \-\-db " " FILE
Use FILE as the SQLite session database.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the AIDebug version and exit.
.SH EXAMPLE
.B aidebug --binary sample.exe --offline --no-tui --report --json-export --out-dir reports/
.PP
Remote AI analysis requires the optional AI dependencies and an Anthropic API
key, and sends sample-derived evidence to the configured model. Bulk remote
analysis also requires
.BR \-\-accept-ai-cost .
See the upstream safety documentation before use.
.SH SAFETY
Use only in an isolated malware-analysis VM or authorized lab. Do not execute
unknown samples on a host operating system.
